WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.168 --> 00:00:08.210
MAX SACK: My name is Max.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.950
This is my colleague Greg.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.300
And we are both program managers
on our billing support team.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.860
So what do we have in
store for this session?

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.620
Well, we'll start
with an overview

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.570
of resource organization
and access management.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:26.020
We'll talk about what that
is, why it's important,

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.310
what resources are involved,
and how those resources relate

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.870
to one another.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.610
We'll then spend some
time taking a look

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.660
at each resource in a
little bit more detail,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.210
as well as the
key roles that are

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.360
necessary to manage
those resources,

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.670
and share some of those
key configuration best

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.160
practices to think about when
you're actually setting those

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.480
up.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.980
We'll then take a
little bit of time

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.830
and look at an example
of how the organization

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.860
of those resources as well
as the role assignments

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.830
will evolve over time for
an organization that's

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.000
growing in size.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.400
And then we'll wrap up.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.040
We don't have a ton of time
to talk to you all today

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.599
and cover this content in a
detail that we would like.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:07.680
And so, we're going to point you
to some key documentation that

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.440
will go into a lot more
detail, a lot more depth,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.970
that you guys can use with
your teams after the fact,

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.770
and actually step by step
go through this process

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.570
when you're configuring
your accounts.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.780
Resource Organization
and Access Management.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:22.900
It's a bit of a mouthful.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.900
So we're just going to
refer to this as resource

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.380
management for simplicity.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.290
OK, resource management--
what is this?

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.250
In the context of
cloud, the term resource

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.390
is thrown around a lot.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.740
You've probably heard it
here at the conference.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.260
We felt it would be pretty
helpful to define what

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.300
we mean by resource and
resource management,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.600
first by talking about
what we don't mean.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.540
In the context of GCP when
people hear the term resource,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.940
they often think of
those service resources

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.720
that are used to
process your workloads--

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.920
your VMs, your databases, all
of the underlying products

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.480
and services that you
consume using GCP.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.930
But that's not really
what we're talking about.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.767
When we talk about
resource management,

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:04.350
we're talking about
the resources that

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.200
sit above those services,
the ones that are involved

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.680
and actually managing and
configuring your accounts,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.690
such as those you see here.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.690
Now, I want to note that
we're not explicitly

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.640
talking about or looking
at these resources

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.150
through the lens
of cost management,

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.130
such as how you can or
should be organizing

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:26.100
your various resources,
your projects, your folders,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.670
your labels, for purposes of
separating out and tracking

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.530
your various cost
considerations.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.300
That's a super
important topic, and one

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.092
that's really well-covered
by our colleague

00:02:35.092 --> 00:02:38.310
James and his session on
resource organization for cost

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:41.530
management, which
he gave yesterday.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.800
So if you didn't get a
chance to check that one out,

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:44.682
that's totally fine.

00:02:44.682 --> 00:02:46.140
That session and
all the other ones

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.467
will be available on
YouTube after Next.

00:02:48.467 --> 00:02:50.550
And we highly encourage
you to check that one out,

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.467
because it's actually a
really nice compliment

00:02:52.467 --> 00:02:54.000
to what we want to cover here.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.110
Our purposes in this
session are to give you

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.930
some prescriptive guidance
on how you should be thinking

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.540
about these resources and
the roles that are associated

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.860
with them so that you can
configure account and maintain

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.830
good account hygiene.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.690
That's really our purpose here.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:15.250
So why is this important?

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.750
Not configuring an
account correctly

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:20.290
can lead to some really
common and problematic states

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.710
that impact your team's
ability to use GCP.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.290
You may be wondering, why are
we up here from the support

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.450
team talking to you about
account configuration

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.710
and resource setup?

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.410
The reason for that is, when
customers don't do those things

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:36.215
correctly, it often impacts
their billing, their invoicing,

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:38.590
their payments, and they reach
out to the billing support

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.278
team for help.

00:03:40.278 --> 00:03:42.820
So we're really well positioned
to see all the different ways

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.800
that this can go wrong based
on the upstream decisions

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.050
that our customers are making.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:48.592
So we thought we
were in a good place

00:03:48.592 --> 00:03:51.333
to provide some of this guidance
and sort of fill in those gaps

00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:53.500
when you are making those
decisions to sort of guide

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.543
you through that.

00:03:54.543 --> 00:03:56.710
It's pretty telling here
actually that about a third

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:59.530
of all the customer cases,
the customer inquiries coming

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:02.260
into the billing support
team, are related in some way

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.760
to resource management.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.790
So it's super common, affects
really large customers

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:09.410
and really small
customers as well.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:10.637
What's at stake?

00:04:10.637 --> 00:04:12.470
What happens if you
don't do this correctly?

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.045
What can actually go wrong?

00:04:15.045 --> 00:04:16.670
These problems with
resource management

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.200
can manifest in
a number of ways,

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.860
ranging from an inability
to retrieve information

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.790
from your accounts, to changing
settings on your resources,

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.970
billing accounts,
projects, to losing access

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.900
to those resources completely,
all the way up to disruption

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.160
of service and termination
of your account,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.903
which unfortunately
has actually happened.

00:04:37.903 --> 00:04:40.320
Oftentimes, though, it's some
combination of these things.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.590
It's a garbled mess of
problems sort of stacked

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.360
on top of each other,
and to fix them

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.158
requires sort of untangling
this ball of yarn.

00:04:48.158 --> 00:04:49.700
So we thought the
best way to address

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.460
these issues was to help you
avoid them to begin with.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:55.700
All right.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.283
Let's zoom out a little bit and
take a look at these resources

00:04:58.283 --> 00:05:00.830
and how they relate to
each other in this resource

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:01.778
hierarchy.

00:05:01.778 --> 00:05:03.320
Starting at the top
in the red boxes,

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.820
we have the domain
and the organization.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.420
These things collectively
create this sort of umbrella

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.480
that lets you more
easily manage your users

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:15.360
as well as your resources
within the organization.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.460
In the green box, we
have the billing account,

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.300
which is responsible for
tracking all of your charges

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.780
that you're accruing as
you use GCP services,

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.140
and helps define how you pay for
those services in combination

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.640
with the payment profile, which
is the yellow box over there

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.940
on the left.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.760
Uniquely, the payment profile
is not a cloud-level resource.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.720
It sits outside of that
resource hierarchy,

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.307
and it is responsible
for, among other things,

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:43.640
holding and actually containing
your payment instrument.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:45.770
So if you use a credit
card or debit card,

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.310
that would reside within
your payment profile.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:50.930
Lastly, we have the blue boxes--

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:53.442
projects, folders, and labels.

00:05:53.442 --> 00:05:55.400
And collectively, you
can think of these things

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:57.500
as this flexible
mechanism that helps

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.350
you manage not only your
cost, but also your access

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:01.850
to the various
underlying services

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.570
that you're going to be using.

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:05.070
All right.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.060
So let's dig into these
a little bit more.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:10.050
I want to start by noting
each of your teams,

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:12.792
each of your companies, is going
to have various considerations

00:06:12.792 --> 00:06:14.250
when you're setting
up your account

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.190
and setting up these resources--
business considerations,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.290
financial, legal,
organizational.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.573
That's totally fine.

00:06:20.573 --> 00:06:22.740
Those things are going to
influence the way that you

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.115
actually know the decisions
you make when you're actually

00:06:25.115 --> 00:06:26.760
configuring and
setting this stuff up.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.680
What we want to focus
on is providing you

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.560
with some common best
practices and a general order

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.140
of operations that you
should be thinking about when

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.917
you configure these resources.

00:06:35.917 --> 00:06:37.500
Everyone's is going
to look different,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.270
but there are some
common things that we

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.160
want to encourage you to do.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:43.200
In this section, we'll
introduce each resource,

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.533
we'll talk about what of those
key configuration details

00:06:45.533 --> 00:06:47.190
and best practices
are, and we will

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:48.600
introduce the core
roles that are

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.162
necessary to manage each one.

00:06:52.162 --> 00:06:54.860
So let's start with
domains and organizations.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:56.480
Like we said,
together these create

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:59.690
this umbrella under which
all of your cloud resources

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.825
are nested, helps you manage
those resources and your users.

00:07:02.825 --> 00:07:04.700
These things are different,
domains and orgs,

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.362
but they map on a
one-to-one basis.

00:07:06.362 --> 00:07:07.820
So you can sort of
think about them

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:11.360
as two sides of the same coin.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.120
A good way to conceptually
break these out

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:18.308
is think about domain as the
mechanism to manage your users,

00:07:18.308 --> 00:07:19.850
and the organization
is the mechanism

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:24.230
to manage your resources as well
as the access to the resources.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:27.050
Starting with domains-- in the
same way that your web domain

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.860
sort of names your website, your
domain in the context of cloud

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:33.470
names your organization,
and it's at this level

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.720
that you can define
that directory of users

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:37.820
you have within
your organization.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:39.830
You can enforce a
universal policy

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.850
across those users such as
two factor authentication,

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.080
and you can even help
your users recover access

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.970
to their accounts
if they lose access

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.520
and they need to reset
their password, for example.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.850
All that stuff can be managed
within the Google Admin

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:54.438
Console.

00:07:54.438 --> 00:07:56.480
When you create a domain,
that will automatically

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.660
create your organization,
like I said, one-to-one.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:01.640
We'll talk about how
to do that in a moment.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.030
On the organization
side, you can

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.190
think about this as that root
node in your GCP hierarchy.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:08.870
It's by virtue of
this that you have

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.870
control over all of those
subordinate resources that

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.698
sit underneath the org.

00:08:14.698 --> 00:08:16.740
This is really important
for a couple of reasons.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.360
The first one is
proactive management.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.300
So if your company is
expanding, if you're

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:21.675
going to acquire
another company,

00:08:21.675 --> 00:08:24.290
if you're going to
spin up a new division,

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.310
if you didn't have
an organization,

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.745
you would have to individually
communicate and coordinate

00:08:28.745 --> 00:08:30.620
between all of the
individual resource owners

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:31.940
to achieve some larger vision.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:34.442
If you had an organization,
it makes that really easy

00:08:34.442 --> 00:08:35.900
because you can
sort of quarterback

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.370
those changes from
a central point,

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.245
and that goes really quickly.

00:08:40.245 --> 00:08:41.870
The second reason
it's really important

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:43.228
is for reactive management.

00:08:43.228 --> 00:08:45.770
If you have a really important
project that you're working on

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:47.930
and only a single employee,
key employee, that's

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.810
managing that project,
and they win the lottery

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:53.950
and never come into work
again, you're out of luck

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:55.560
if you don't have
an organization.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.018
You have to get in
touch with them,

00:08:57.018 --> 00:09:00.350
sort of beg them to add
somebody else to the project.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:01.218
It's kind of a mess.

00:09:01.218 --> 00:09:03.260
If you have an organization,
it sort of functions

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:04.055
as an insurance policy.

00:09:04.055 --> 00:09:06.013
It allows you to really
easily go back in there

00:09:06.013 --> 00:09:10.130
and assign that project to
another person on your team.

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:12.110
All this stuff with
the organization

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:13.940
is related to your
GCP resources.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:19.330
So logically you can control
that using the GCP console.

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:21.620
So how do we create this?

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:23.960
How should we think about
sort of setting this stuff up?

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.960
The first best practice
that we have for you all

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:28.067
is to use a domain
and an organization.

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:29.150
It's completely voluntary.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:32.917
There's no forcing
you to actually use

00:09:32.917 --> 00:09:33.750
one of these things.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:35.225
So we want encourage
you to use it.

00:09:35.225 --> 00:09:37.100
There's not really a
good reason not to based

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:39.240
on what we just discussed.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.930
So to set up a domain
and an organization,

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:43.660
you'll start by
creating the domain.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.320
You have a couple
options to do this.

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:48.800
You can use G Suite or you
can use Cloud Identity.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:50.790
We won't go into the
details of these,

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:52.040
but you have a couple options.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:53.570
They're just great.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:55.677
One of them is free.

00:09:55.677 --> 00:09:57.260
Once you've set that
up, the next step

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.360
will be to provision your users,
and you have a couple options

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:00.270
here as well.

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:02.975
You can do this individually
at the Google Admin console,

00:10:02.975 --> 00:10:04.850
or you can use a tool,
which we would suggest

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:08.000
if you have a large user
base already, Cloud Directory

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.130
Sync, if you want to
import a bunch of users

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:12.765
and provisioned them em masse.

00:10:12.765 --> 00:10:14.640
Once you've set that
up, you'll sort of shift

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.260
focus over to the organization.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.810
Like we said, when you
create your domain,

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.830
it will automatically
create your organization.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:23.992
So you'll shift over to that
GCP console and you will migrate

00:10:23.992 --> 00:10:25.450
any projects that
you've been using

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:26.970
if you've happened
to be using GCP

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:29.100
prior to setting up your work--

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:31.230
project and billing.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.440
Here again, if you have a
large number of resources

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:34.350
that you're going
to be migrating,

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:36.990
we highly suggest you use
the organization setup wizard

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.130
to streamline that process.

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:43.290
OK, lastly-- so at this
point, you have your domain,

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:47.370
you'll have your org, you have
users, you have your resources.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:49.770
Now you're going to sort of
connect the dots and grant

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:55.000
permissions for those
resources to those users.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:57.750
And here, we want to call this
out, really important tip--

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.940
by default leave
the billing account

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.870
creator roles on
your organization

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:03.540
for everyone within it.

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.670
We want to strongly
encourage you to remove that,

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:06.810
to turn that off.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:08.940
And the reason for
that is it will

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.007
lead to a proliferation
of billing accounts

00:11:11.007 --> 00:11:13.340
if you don't do that, and it
becomes really problematic,

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:15.760
and you see a lot of different
ways that this goes wrong.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:18.120
There's not really a good
reason to have that turned on.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.120
You want to restrict
the access to who

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.170
is able to create billing
accounts because of all

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.170
the tie-ins to other resources,
like payment profiles, which

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.967
we'll discuss in a moment,
and it's best to sort of just

00:11:27.967 --> 00:11:29.300
get ahead of that and remove it.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:34.120
So what are the
key roles involved

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:37.780
in setting up and managing
your domain and organization?

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.360
On the domain side we
have the super admin.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:42.250
This person has the
ability to administer

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:43.870
that directory of users.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.650
They are the person that
will recover accounts,

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:49.150
and if your users lose
access and get locked out,

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:52.390
they can reset those passwords.

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:56.965
They can also enforce that sort
of domain-wide policy like 2FA,

00:11:56.965 --> 00:12:01.870
and they can grant the
org admin privilege.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.990
On the other side
with the organization,

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:05.472
you have the org admin.

00:12:05.472 --> 00:12:07.180
This person has the
ability to administer

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:08.410
all of those resources.

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:10.702
They're also the individual
that will do that migration

00:12:10.702 --> 00:12:12.088
if that becomes necessary.

00:12:12.088 --> 00:12:14.380
And it's worth noting that
by default, the first domain

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.380
super admin, the person that
actually sets up the domain,

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:21.830
will also be the
first org admin.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:25.880
And a couple of notes on who
typically fills these roles

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.590
within an organization, it can
be very different depending

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:29.840
on the size your organization.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.690
When you're first
starting out, very common

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.220
had the same individual across
both of these roles, someone

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:38.270
that has IT responsibilities
for your team, for your company.

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:40.160
As organizations get
larger, typically we

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.270
see org admins sort
of peel off from that,

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:44.990
and might be someone
more directly involved

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.600
in the use of cloud
services at your company.

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:49.887
Could be a CTO, head of
product, head of engineering,

00:12:49.887 --> 00:12:50.720
something like that.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:56.430
Quick note on roles.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.282
This applies not
only to the roles

00:12:58.282 --> 00:13:00.240
we just discussed, but
also the ones that we're

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:04.470
going to discuss for
the other resources.

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:06.683
Avoid single points of
failure, like that situation

00:13:06.683 --> 00:13:09.100
we just talked about with the
employee that wins the lotto

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.610
and never comes back.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:15.215
It's a good idea to assign
multiple individuals

00:13:15.215 --> 00:13:17.340
to these key roles, especially
administrator roles,

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:21.450
in practice what we're
calling reasonable redundancy.

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:22.630
That's half the battle.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.470
You also want to make sure that
every other individual, all

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:27.510
the other people
on your team, know

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:30.210
who those administrators
are, because they oftentimes

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:32.250
have special privileges
and permissions

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:35.263
and access within your
company and within GCP.

00:13:35.263 --> 00:13:37.680
And so, they sort of function
as its first line of defense

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.020
when problems come up, and
they can help troubleshoot.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:41.220
So it's really important.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:43.868
We see this all the time
in larger organizations.

00:13:43.868 --> 00:13:45.660
People don't know who
the billing admin is.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:47.370
People don't know
who the org admin is.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:48.912
And they're coming
to support, and it

00:13:48.912 --> 00:13:50.450
creates this sort
of telephone game,

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:53.392
and it gets pretty complicated.

00:13:53.392 --> 00:13:55.350
And then, lastly, we know
we know teams change,

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:58.180
we know companies evolve, people
leave, and people come in.

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.900
And so, sort of kick the tires
on this rolls and permissions

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:03.232
list every six months,
or every quarter even,

00:14:03.232 --> 00:14:04.440
to make sure it's up to date.

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:09.840
All right, billing accounts--

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.820
So as we said before,
the billing account

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:15.240
is responsible for containing
all of your charges,

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:18.060
tracking all of your charges
from the usage of your GCP

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.190
services.

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:21.930
And it's all those charges that
hit that billing account that

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:24.778
ultimately make its
way onto your invoice.

00:14:24.778 --> 00:14:26.820
So there is one invoice
for every billing account

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.380
that you have.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:30.760
The billing account also
is responsible for defining

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:32.760
how you will pay for that
invoice in combination

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:36.490
with the payment profile,
which we'll discuss shortly.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.940
And like all of the other
resources we're talking about,

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:40.465
has its own roles
and permissions.

00:14:40.465 --> 00:14:42.090
And that's kind of
the core reason, one

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.400
of the core reasons, why
things get really dicey when

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:45.317
setting this stuff up.

00:14:45.317 --> 00:14:48.090
Because if you're doing it in a
silo in a way that's not really

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:49.840
paying attention to
how you're configuring

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:51.632
your other resources
and who you're putting

00:14:51.632 --> 00:14:55.020
in those key roles, it
creates a lot of confusion

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:58.123
within customers' companies.

00:14:58.123 --> 00:15:00.540
Also important to note here
that billing accounts can only

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:05.440
operate in a single currency
per billing account.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.770
So how should you configure
a billing account?

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:11.860
The first step is to decide how
many billing accounts you need.

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:13.760
And this is kind
of a trick point,

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:16.030
because we want to strongly
encourage you to have

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:17.593
only a single billing account.

00:15:17.593 --> 00:15:20.260
That will be appropriate for the
vast majority of our customers,

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:22.990
and probably most the
folks in this room.

00:15:22.990 --> 00:15:26.730
We see oftentimes customers will
use multiple billing accounts

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:28.680
as sort of a hack
for cost management

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.890
if they want to
separate out costs.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:32.440
But we have other
great resources

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:34.480
like projects,
folders, and labels,

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:36.970
that we should really be
relying on for the heavy lifting

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.270
for all of our cost
management practices,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:42.592
and let billing accounts kind
of just be billing accounts.

00:15:42.592 --> 00:15:44.050
There are a couple
situations where

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:46.630
it does make sense to have
multiple billing accounts.

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:48.940
If you operate separate
legal entities,

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:50.680
if you operate in
different geographies

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:53.320
and different currencies, in
those cases it will make sense

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:55.403
and you will need to have
separate billing counts.

00:15:55.403 --> 00:15:57.620
But otherwise keep it to one.

00:15:57.620 --> 00:15:59.620
The reason for that,
again, with a proliferation

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:01.935
of billing accounts, it
creates a lot of overhead

00:16:01.935 --> 00:16:04.060
to make sure that you're
tracking things correctly,

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.360
that you have permission
syncs correctly,

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:09.460
if you have preferential pricing
it creates a lot of problems.

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:12.290
So we strongly suggest
keep it to one.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:12.790
All right.

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:14.750
So first step is deciding
how many you need,

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:17.110
but also making sure
that it's just one.

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:20.900
The second step is defining and
creating that billing account.

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:22.510
So if you've already
been using GCP,

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:24.927
you can choose the one that's
going to be the primary one.

00:16:24.927 --> 00:16:27.020
If you haven't pre-set
up your main one,

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:28.870
call that your primary.

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:32.500
At this point, we want to call
out as well turn on or enable

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:35.410
BigQuery billing
export on day one

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:37.270
when you create your
billing account.

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:38.690
You can't do this retroactively.

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:41.328
And so, to make sure that you
have as much data as possible

00:16:41.328 --> 00:16:43.120
you have access to,
it's always a good idea

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:47.130
to turn it on as soon as
you've created the account.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:50.390
Next, you're going to migrate
any resources, any projects

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:53.240
specifically, over to this
billing account that you

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:55.250
may have been using previously.

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:57.950
And then lastly, you're going
to settle any final balances you

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:00.325
have on older billing accounts
you are no longer planning

00:17:00.325 --> 00:17:02.245
on using and close them down.

00:17:02.245 --> 00:17:03.620
We've seen this
quite a bit where

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.359
a customer will
have a stray billing

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:07.280
account with a single
project attached to it,

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:10.743
a little bit of usage, and
the credit card associated

00:17:10.743 --> 00:17:12.410
with that one sort
of lapses and then it

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:15.060
creates account
closure protocols,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:16.500
and that has a domino effect.

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:18.950
It ends up affecting their
other live billing accounts.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:21.560
And so, it's really best
to just shut those down

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.327
if you have any
extra ones, and just

00:17:23.327 --> 00:17:24.910
focus on that primary
billing account.

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:28.800
All right.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:32.370
Key roles in managing
your billing account--

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:34.290
So the billing
account administrator

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.185
is going to be the
primary role here.

00:17:36.185 --> 00:17:38.310
This person has the ability
to manage those payment

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:39.270
instruments.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:41.940
They can enable BigQuery export,
like we just talked about

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:43.470
as the best practice.

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:44.970
They can view costs.

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:49.210
They can also setup cost
reports or budget alerts,

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:51.360
which are great cost
management tools that you

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:52.980
should be taking advantage of.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.805
They can link projects,
unlink projects,

00:17:55.805 --> 00:17:57.180
and manage all of
the other users

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:59.760
and permissions are related
to that billing account.

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.130
Really important here
like we talked about--

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.710
certain administrators
of certain resources

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.390
have special
privileges and rights.

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:09.570
And so, for the billion count
specifically, we require--

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:11.310
we as in the building
support team--

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:13.440
require any individuals
reaching out to us

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:16.890
to ask about a billing
account, that that person be

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:20.520
a billing account administrator
on that particular account.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.310
The reason for that is to
protect our customer's privacy.

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:25.560
We don't want to
disclose any information

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.380
to an individual that
wouldn't otherwise have access

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:29.777
to that information.

00:18:29.777 --> 00:18:31.860
So again, make sure that
you have multiple billing

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:34.197
administrators, and
also let everyone

00:18:34.197 --> 00:18:35.280
know who those people are.

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.120
If they have a
question about billing,

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.453
they're going to need to
funnel that through the billing

00:18:39.453 --> 00:18:42.082
administrator.

00:18:42.082 --> 00:18:43.290
We have another role up here.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:44.832
And there's more
than just these two,

00:18:44.832 --> 00:18:47.170
but we thought these were
probably the most relevant.

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:48.795
The billing account
user-- this person,

00:18:48.795 --> 00:18:50.560
a lot more limited in scope.

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:53.630
They can basically view costs
for the billing account,

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:55.980
and they can link projects
into the billing account,

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:57.510
but they can't unlink them.

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.460
So this role's often
granted in tandem

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:01.080
with a project creator role.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:04.500
So it gives the individual the
ability to create a project

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:07.440
and then fund that project
by connecting it back

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.840
into the billing account.

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:11.760
Oftentimes, people
in organizations

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:14.920
that we see filling the billing
account administrator role

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:17.970
are folks that are working
in finance and accounting,

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.850
or oftentimes people that
have PNL responsibility

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:22.270
for a particular product line.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:26.760
So it could be someone
in product management.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:27.480
All right.

00:19:27.480 --> 00:19:29.010
And with that, I'm going
to pass things over

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.718
to my colleague Greg,
who's going to talk

00:19:30.718 --> 00:19:32.040
to us about payment profiles.

00:19:34.352 --> 00:19:35.310
GREG GULLEN: All right.

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:36.210
So thank you, Max.

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:37.830
So once again, my
name is Greg Gullen,

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:40.930
and I'm a program manager
on the billing support team.

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:43.740
So before we even talking
about payments profiles,

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.570
just a quick show of
hands, how many of you

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:47.895
have actually heard of
this resource before?

00:19:47.895 --> 00:19:50.490
Just a quick show of hands.

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:52.310
What about the Google
Payment Center?

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:57.410
Literally, I think I
see like three hands.

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:58.180
So this is great.

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.110
This is great, because
as you can see,

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:03.488
the payments profile sits
outside of your GCP resources.

00:20:03.488 --> 00:20:05.530
And you need to understand
why this is important,

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:07.822
because there is a huge
connection between the payments

00:20:07.822 --> 00:20:10.150
profile and the billing account.

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:11.890
We'll talk about
why it's important

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:14.930
and why you should
take note of it.

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:17.260
So the payments
profile is actually

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:19.960
managed in a completely
different console.

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:22.190
It's not managed
in the GCP console.

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:25.090
If you go to
Payments.Google.com,

00:20:25.090 --> 00:20:28.660
you will actually see what
the Payment Center looks like.

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:31.150
I want to include a screenshot
because I want you all

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:34.630
to be very well aware that the
payments profile permissions

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:37.300
and notifications are
handled completely

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:38.890
outside the GCP console.

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:42.710
There is some sections that are
iframed into the GCP console,

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:45.310
chaining certain
email preferences,

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:47.590
but however, the
majority of settings

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:50.130
should be handled that
Payments.Google.com.

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:59.150
So the payments profile is
a Google-level resource,

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:01.400
and what that means is that
you utilize the payments

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:06.860
profile to pay for your Google
services beyond just GCP.

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:08.360
So you can use your
payments profile

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:15.860
to pay for things like AdWords,
GCP, Chrome, Google Play.

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.380
As it's a Google-level
resource, you

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:20.570
are able to manage
different payment methods

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:22.080
within that payment profile.

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:23.870
So if you're an
online customer, you

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:27.320
can manage things such as debit
cards, credit cards, or bank

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:30.460
account information.

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:32.170
Within the Payment
Center, think of it

00:21:32.170 --> 00:21:35.260
as a holistic location to
view a lot of your invoices

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:36.010
and documents.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:39.160
There's an actual section
called the document center

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:42.670
where you can see credit
memos, debit memos, and master

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:43.803
agreements.

00:21:43.803 --> 00:21:45.970
But the thing to take away
from the payments profile

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:49.090
is that it is managed completely
separate outside of your GCP

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:50.890
console, and I think
a lot of customers

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:52.240
are not aware of this.

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:53.740
So we'll talk about
the implications

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.140
of what it means to
configure your payments

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:57.580
profile correctly,
and how it can

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:01.230
be successful for
your organization.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.800
So when you're thinking
about the payments profile,

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:08.000
think of these four steps to
follow our best practices.

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:14.090
Step one-- create a
business payments profile.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:16.370
A business payments
profile allows

00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:19.640
you to go ahead and enter
your tax information.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:22.370
We are assuming that
your organization

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:26.390
is a business is treated
as a separate legal entity.

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:31.140
If you were to create an
individual payments profile,

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:33.140
you would not have
that functionality.

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:35.840
But most importantly is that
the business payments profile

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:38.570
allows you to manage a lot
of the permissions and email

00:22:38.570 --> 00:22:41.690
notifications for all of
your users in your company.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:46.520
That doesn't happen with the
individual payments profile.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:50.720
We recommend that you limit
it to one, if possible.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:52.580
Very similar to
billing accounts, what

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:55.160
we've seen in billing
support is that whenever

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:58.280
customers create multiple
payments profiles

00:22:58.280 --> 00:23:00.920
and they call in to
GCP support, it's

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.320
very hard to diagnose
where the issue lies,

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:06.470
in which payment profile, in
which billing account could

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:09.560
be the root or
could allow access

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:10.890
to troubleshoot that issue.

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.250
So we recommend limit to one.

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:14.900
There are different
scenarios where

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:17.457
you would have multiple
payments profiles,

00:23:17.457 --> 00:23:19.040
and we'll walk through
those scenarios

00:23:19.040 --> 00:23:21.140
later on in the presentation.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:25.940
Step two-- setup your payments
profile administrator.

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:29.240
Now, we recommend that you
mirror this payments profile

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:31.220
administrator to be
your billing account

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:34.062
administrator, the exact
same permissions if possible,

00:23:34.062 --> 00:23:35.270
for the reason that I stated.

00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:38.420
It's that if you call in two
billing support today, one

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:42.920
of the ways that we
authenticate and verify

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:45.590
you is that we ask you if
you are the billing account

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:46.640
administrator.

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:49.197
Now, if the issue does lie
with the payments profile,

00:23:49.197 --> 00:23:50.780
it's always best to
ensure that you're

00:23:50.780 --> 00:23:52.880
the administrator of
both of those resources

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:57.750
so we can troubleshoot
the issue from end to end.

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:00.570
Step three-- configure users
to receive invoices and account

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:02.348
notifications.

00:24:02.348 --> 00:24:04.140
We can't stress enough
how many times we've

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:06.750
seen fire drills
for customers where

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:08.970
you've got someone
in accounts payable

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:10.860
and you have someone
in engineering,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:13.260
and they're receiving different
types of notifications

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:14.690
based on the account.

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:16.440
And when that happens,
there's fire drills

00:24:16.440 --> 00:24:18.780
within organizations
as to why is my bill

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:20.940
not being paid, what's
going on, how come

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:23.370
I can't access these resources.

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:25.980
So ensure that
everybody is receiving

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:29.160
the right level
of notifications,

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:32.880
and more importantly,
can pay the bill.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:36.220
Step four-- add a
secondary payment method.

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:39.120
So we've seen historically
that credit cards

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:42.030
for online accounts,
credit cards expire.

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:45.120
Individuals leave
organizations, and in doing so,

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:48.000
sometimes your GCP
resources do not get paid.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:51.400
So we recommend please add
a secondary payment method.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:54.480
So if the first one
fails you have a backup.

00:24:54.480 --> 00:24:59.040
For offline accounts, also known
as terms or invoice accounts,

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:02.040
we recommend that you have
a secondary individual setup

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:04.350
as a payments profile
administrator.

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:06.450
And not only that, but
you also take a look,

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:09.990
log into the center, and you
can actually assign individuals

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:12.900
within your organization,
such as finance, accounting,

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:14.480
to just receive
the invoice itself.

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:20.060
We recommend that you review
this every six months.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.717
It's just good hygiene to have.

00:25:21.717 --> 00:25:23.300
Like I said, we
understand that people

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:25.948
go on and do great things
and leave organizations.

00:25:25.948 --> 00:25:28.490
So please just make sure this
information is kept up to date.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:34.480
So the payments profile
has two important roles

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:35.590
you should take note of.

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:37.900
One is the payments
provide administrator.

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:40.070
This individual has the
keys to the kingdom.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:42.640
This individual can go ahead
and change the actual business

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:46.960
name, the legal address,
different payment methods,

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:49.060
change the entire
notifications for everyone

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:52.370
receiving invoice information.

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:53.980
So it's important
that this individual

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:55.750
within the
organization is someone

00:25:55.750 --> 00:26:01.330
who usually has senior level
visibility into not only what

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:04.660
your organization is interacting
with Google from a GCP

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:07.840
perspective, but across
our entire portfolio

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:12.770
of different products, like
ads, or Google Play, or Chrome.

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:15.470
Now, if you don't want to
give full access to a user,

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:16.700
we understand that.

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:19.550
You have the option of
the read only access.

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:21.540
Now the read only access,
as the name implies,

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:24.350
is just for someone who just
needs to see the invoices, just

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:26.905
needs to see the
transaction history.

00:26:26.905 --> 00:26:29.030
Historically, what we've
seen is that an individual

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:34.130
with this role will be someone
in accounting, finance,

00:26:34.130 --> 00:26:35.780
or accounts payable.

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:40.150
All right.

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:42.720
So switching gears to
projects, folders, and labels.

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:45.900
So as Max had mentioned before,
projects, folders, and labels

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:50.310
are really a flexible mechanism
to help you with your cost

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:52.365
management needs.

00:26:52.365 --> 00:26:54.990
If they're structured correctly,
they help you answer questions

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:58.470
such as, how am I spending
my GCP resources today,

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:01.440
or where can I
shift my resources

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:02.835
to get the most use out of GCP.

00:27:09.310 --> 00:27:10.810
So projects,
folders, and labels.

00:27:10.810 --> 00:27:12.435
Projects-- if you
had to think of them,

00:27:12.435 --> 00:27:14.020
think of them as a bucket.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:17.770
In this bucket it stores a lot
of service-level resources.

00:27:17.770 --> 00:27:19.870
And these
service-level resources

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:23.560
could be things
such as Kubernetes,

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:27.140
compute, storage, and so on.

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:30.760
Now, on top of those
projects, you have folders,

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:33.820
and the folders allow you
to organize your resources

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:35.290
in a logical way.

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:37.960
What we've seen companies
utilize in a structure

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:40.090
perspective is, you can
structure your folders

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:43.080
to mean things such
as environments--

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:45.940
so your staging environment,
your production environment--

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:47.650
or you can even
structure them to be

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:50.500
actual teams-- so your
engineering team, your support

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:53.580
team, et cetera.

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:57.390
Labels, on the other hand, are
great for being able to track

00:27:57.390 --> 00:27:59.502
your costs across GCP.

00:27:59.502 --> 00:28:00.960
If applied correctly,
they'll allow

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:03.002
you to answer a lot of
your fundamental questions

00:28:03.002 --> 00:28:06.390
as to how much is application
A spending from a maintenance

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:08.933
perspective or from a
development perspective.

00:28:08.933 --> 00:28:10.350
So we encourage
you to use labels.

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:18.110
So when setting up
these resources,

00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:20.010
we recommend these four things.

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:23.990
One is organize your
folders and projects

00:28:23.990 --> 00:28:26.240
to mirror your organization.

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:29.000
We can't stress enough-- please
use a naming convention that

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:31.010
works for your organization.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.940
While I personally think naming
a project CoolRider87 makes

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:37.820
sense, someone in
finance might not

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:40.230
be able to tie that purchase
order to that invoice

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:42.810
and might not be able to
make sense of that project.

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:46.880
So use something that makes
sense for your organization.

00:28:46.880 --> 00:28:50.860
Step two-- protect projects
that are mission critical.

00:28:50.860 --> 00:28:54.130
We appreciate that all of you
have placed a lot of trust

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:57.430
onto GCP to run your
mission critical projects.

00:28:57.430 --> 00:29:00.340
In order to respect that, we've
created a functionality called

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:02.350
placing a lien on a project.

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:03.970
When you place a
lien on a project,

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:06.860
it prevents it from
accidental deletion.

00:29:06.860 --> 00:29:09.172
This can be done
through an API call,

00:29:09.172 --> 00:29:11.380
or it could even be done
through a command line tool.

00:29:14.070 --> 00:29:18.660
Step three-- add labels for
more granular cost management.

00:29:18.660 --> 00:29:20.790
Now, as we said before,
if you apply labels

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:22.650
at the right level
of resources, you

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:25.140
can answer some of your
most basic questions.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:29.880
You can even answer as to how
much are my database costs--

00:29:29.880 --> 00:29:31.740
how much am I incurring
from a database cost

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:33.660
perspective across
all my projects,

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:36.510
or specific to just one project?

00:29:36.510 --> 00:29:39.030
When creating these resources,
these GCP resources,

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:41.580
we recommend that through the
workflow you actually-- there's

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:44.130
a section where you can add
a label to that resource

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:45.570
early on.

00:29:45.570 --> 00:29:48.390
Now, if you already have your
hierarchy set up, that's OK.

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:51.000
You can actually retroactively
go back and apply those labels

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:51.990
to those resources.

00:29:54.530 --> 00:29:59.830
Step four-- assign appropriate
roles for folders and projects.

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:03.640
We recommend that you use Cloud
Identity and Access Management,

00:30:03.640 --> 00:30:05.500
also known as Cloud IAM.

00:30:05.500 --> 00:30:08.290
So Cloud IAM allows
you to centrally

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:11.350
manage all of these permissions
and identities and roles

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:13.085
all at once.

00:30:13.085 --> 00:30:14.710
As an administrator
it's very difficult

00:30:14.710 --> 00:30:16.810
to be applying and
removing permissions

00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:18.170
per each individual.

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:20.710
But with Cloud IAM you
could do it holistically.

00:30:20.710 --> 00:30:23.350
But most importantly is
that Cloud IAM allows you

00:30:23.350 --> 00:30:26.090
for audit and tracking purposes.

00:30:26.090 --> 00:30:28.150
So for your
organization, it might

00:30:28.150 --> 00:30:29.620
be very important
to know who made

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:35.210
the changes, who gave individual
X, Y, permission and so on.

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:38.697
So it's good from a security
and compliance standpoint.

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:45.950
Now, with project folders and
labels, we have two roles--

00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:47.860
one being the
project creator role,

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:50.150
and the second being
the project editor role.

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:52.760
As the name implies,
the project creator

00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:54.765
allows you to create projects.

00:30:54.765 --> 00:30:57.140
This individual will not only
be able to create projects,

00:30:57.140 --> 00:30:59.420
but will also manage all
of the service resources

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:01.780
underneath it.

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:03.800
At organizations, we've
seen that an individual

00:31:03.800 --> 00:31:06.530
with his permission
usually is in charge

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:08.120
of developing the application.

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:13.310
So it could be anyone from an
engineering lead to a project

00:31:13.310 --> 00:31:18.170
manager, but someone who
has access and visibility

00:31:18.170 --> 00:31:22.030
into budget cycles and so on.

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:24.670
The second role is the
project editor role.

00:31:24.670 --> 00:31:27.430
Now, if you don't want to
give full permissions of being

00:31:27.430 --> 00:31:30.207
able to create projects willy
nilly around the organization,

00:31:30.207 --> 00:31:32.290
you can go ahead and give
the project editor role.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:34.082
And as the name implies,
you can go ahead--

00:31:34.082 --> 00:31:37.960
the project editor allows
you to manage and edit

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:40.430
the resources
underneath that project.

00:31:40.430 --> 00:31:44.560
So this individual,
usually at organizations,

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:48.115
is someone such as a developer
or a project manager.

00:31:52.540 --> 00:31:54.340
So Max and I spent
a great deal of time

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:57.550
talking about all these
different resources, right?

00:31:57.550 --> 00:32:00.190
And we figured that it would
be important to provide

00:32:00.190 --> 00:32:02.320
some concrete
examples as to how you

00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:07.380
can follow these best practices
for your organization.

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:09.600
We're actually going to walk
through a startup called

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:11.360
Digital D0nut.

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.400
Now, Digital D0nut
has figured out a way

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:16.460
how to break down donuts
into zeros and ones

00:32:16.460 --> 00:32:18.260
and send them over
the internet--

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:22.160
completely revolutionary,
and they're making waves.

00:32:22.160 --> 00:32:24.470
So what you see in this
organizational structure

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:27.900
is that it's very basic
to follow a startup.

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:31.700
You've got one
organization, one project,

00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:33.875
some service resources
underneath it,

00:32:33.875 --> 00:32:35.750
and a billing account
and a payments profile.

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.730
And with every startup, you've
got a couple of individuals

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:45.240
just working really hard
to gain that next customer

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:48.000
and grow the actual business.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.670
So you have a founder
and a developer.

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:55.500
Now the founder ends up becoming
the organizational admin

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:58.980
as well as the billing account
admin and payments profile

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:00.570
admin.

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:03.510
The founder really wanted
to let the developer focus

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:06.000
on what they do best, and that's
developing the application

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:07.500
for Digital D0nut.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:09.870
So as you can see, the
developer becomes the project

00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:12.700
administrator/owner.

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:15.510
And this developer is focused
on building the chocolate

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:17.030
application for Digital D0nut.

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:26.490
So as Digital D0nut has
expanded beyond markets,

00:33:26.490 --> 00:33:28.120
they started
realizing that there

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:31.450
was more of a need than
just chocolate donuts.

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:34.027
The founder ended up working
with some industry analysts

00:33:34.027 --> 00:33:35.860
to go ahead and analyze
their customer base,

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:38.470
and they found that customers
not only love chocolate donuts,

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:40.930
but also jelly-filled donuts.

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:42.430
So as you can see
in this hierarchy,

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:45.520
you've got project
one and project two.

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:48.910
Because the founder decided to
build out a new product line,

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:50.770
they hired an
additional developer

00:33:50.770 --> 00:33:53.770
to become the project
administrator.

00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:56.440
So here, you've separated
the responsibilities

00:33:56.440 --> 00:33:58.060
by product line--

00:33:58.060 --> 00:34:01.600
the chocolate product line
and the jelly product line.

00:34:01.600 --> 00:34:03.460
So project one is chocolate.

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:05.870
Project two would be jelly.

00:34:05.870 --> 00:34:07.730
Now, the founder
has not had a chance

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:09.570
to hire any
additional resources.

00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:11.780
So they remain the
org admin, as well as

00:34:11.780 --> 00:34:14.938
the billing account and
payments profile administrator.

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:21.310
Now, Digital D0nut has not only
expanded from state to state,

00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:23.969
but it's just expanding
all across the country.

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:25.670
And with this expansion,
as you can see,

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:27.795
the organizational structure
is starting to change,

00:34:27.795 --> 00:34:29.818
it's starting to morph.

00:34:29.818 --> 00:34:31.360
You see the introduction
of folders--

00:34:31.360 --> 00:34:33.909
folder one and folder two.

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:38.380
With this new found success, the
founder decided to hire a CTO.

00:34:38.380 --> 00:34:41.530
This Chief Technology Officer
comes from an organization that

00:34:41.530 --> 00:34:44.739
has deep expertise in
business-to-business--

00:34:44.739 --> 00:34:47.679
to handle
business-to-business markets.

00:34:47.679 --> 00:34:49.900
One of things the
CTO started doing

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:52.270
as they came into the
organization was they

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:55.090
wanted to understand and
see if Digital D0nut can

00:34:55.090 --> 00:34:57.852
move beyond a
business-to-consumer market.

00:34:57.852 --> 00:34:59.560
And they found that
there was a huge need

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:03.580
to sell donuts to businesses
at catering events.

00:35:03.580 --> 00:35:07.330
So folder one will be the
business-to-consumer division.

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:12.213
Folder two will be the
business-to-business division.

00:35:12.213 --> 00:35:13.880
The engineer that
started at the company

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:16.410
has now moved up
the ranks, ended up

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:18.180
becoming an
engineering lead, has

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:21.780
a few folks working under
them, and becomes the folder

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:23.040
administrator.

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:24.600
As the folder
administrator, they

00:35:24.600 --> 00:35:27.480
have full rights and access
to all the projects, chocolate

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:30.210
and glazed underneath that, and
all the service-level resources

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:32.460
underneath.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:37.090
But with that growth into
the new business division,

00:35:37.090 --> 00:35:40.510
we hired an engineering manager,
and this engineer manager

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:42.370
is going to be tasked
with building out

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:45.190
the business-to-business
division for Digital D0nut.

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:50.220
They become the folder
two administrator/owner.

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:55.080
Also with this success we hired
a chief financial officer.

00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:57.360
As you can see, the
chief financial officer

00:35:57.360 --> 00:36:00.660
becomes the billing account
administrator and the payments

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:03.500
profile admin.

00:36:03.500 --> 00:36:05.270
With this hire,
the founder wanted

00:36:05.270 --> 00:36:08.240
to really understand
how am I spending my GCP

00:36:08.240 --> 00:36:09.410
resources today.

00:36:09.410 --> 00:36:11.570
I'm growing out all these
different product lines.

00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:13.490
I need to understand
where I'm spending

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:15.250
the majority of my
development costs,

00:36:15.250 --> 00:36:17.370
my maintenance costs, and so on.

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:20.420
So the founder, the CTO,
and CFO got together

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:23.590
and started applying labels.

00:36:23.590 --> 00:36:27.240
As you can see, labels are being
applied at the product level as

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:30.090
well as the service
resource level

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:34.280
underneath to measure the
health of Digital D0nut.

00:36:39.340 --> 00:36:41.810
So Digital D0nut has not only
expand across the country,

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:44.140
but now they're looking
at global markets.

00:36:44.140 --> 00:36:47.470
And with these global
markets, the founder

00:36:47.470 --> 00:36:50.470
and all the leadership started
analyzing which markets would

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:52.300
make sense for Digital D0nut.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:55.600
And they found that there was
a huge need and a huge market

00:36:55.600 --> 00:37:00.190
in the European market.

00:37:00.190 --> 00:37:02.760
So as with this, instead
of introducing a brand

00:37:02.760 --> 00:37:05.700
new product, they decided
to acquire a company who

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:07.110
had a similar business model--

00:37:07.110 --> 00:37:08.570
Digital Croissant.

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:10.830
Now, Digital Croissant
was a great acquisition

00:37:10.830 --> 00:37:14.440
